- `narrative-structure-frameworks.amend-2026-10-23.md` — how three articles of *Communications* 8 (1966) describe a narrative — Barthes's argument that narrative's universality forces a common model, Todorov's definition of meaning as correlation and his time / aspect / mode cut of discourse, and Greimas's one message readable on two isotopies with the protagonist as a personalised mediator — read in the French and turned into design orders, never canon
- `narrative-structure-frameworks.amend-2026-10-23b.md` — where Barthes's five codes sit — the five codes in the S/Z reading sheet's own French names (herméneutique, sémique, symbolique, proaïrétique, gnomique); a semiotician's statement that they are in part a reformulation of the levels of the 1966 Introduction, with the proairetic code a synthesis of two of those levels; and the 1966 article's own hierarchy of levels of description on the one page this file can quote — with the attribution table and the design orders that follow from it, as method, not canon.
- `narrative-structure-frameworks.md` — how narrative structure is theorised and specified as a build order for an invented culture — the structuralist premise that narrative is a formal object analysed by levels of description, carried by the Barthes 1966 article at its own page scope, beside a Chinese and Japanese half that is not a translation of that canon: the story/discourse split (故事 as deep structure, 话语 as surface presentation), the actantial model (行动元), the three dimensions of narrative time (次序, 时长, 频率), focalisation and the levels-and-voices problem (查特曼的六种参与者), 起承転結 held as the minimal completable unit of the quest layer rather than as a four-beat plot, a three-layer machine-writable description format (ストーリー, クエスト, シーン, with characters organised on the same three layers), the Japanese narratology of narrative generation (物語生成の物語論) with its cultural-skew argument, and 世界观 kept as tier-C practitioner vocabulary; decision tables and calibration statuses, never a ruling on any culture's story
